\section{Mathematical Nuclear Shell Model}\label{7900011}

\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$; the standard number fields have their usual meanings. All additional conventions are stated locally.


\paragraph{Definitions and notation.}The primary source concerns the shell model of an atom with fermionic electrons, not a nuclear shell model. Electron states are antisymmetric in position and spin. The source's Coulomb Hamiltonian is $H(N,Z)=\sum_{i=1}^N(-\Delta_i-Z/|x_i|)+\sum_{i<j}|x_i-x_j|^{-1}$ on the fermionic space. Shell descriptions group effective one-electron orbitals into energy shells; the question asks how that approximation relates to exact quantum theory.

\paragraph{Catalogue wording retained for traceability.}
Make mathematical sense of the shell model of an atom and its relationship to the exact quantum theory, as in Simon’s original Problem 11.
\paragraph{Scope and source qualification.}The registered title and short catalogue target say nuclear shell model, but the primary source explicitly says shell model of an atom and discusses atomic/chemical physics. The visible source target is corrected to atomic, while the canonical ID and inherited title/status are preserved. The source itself calls the programme vague.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Justify the atomic electron shell model from exact quantum theory.

\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Derived fixed-N large-Z energy and ground-subspace bounds under explicit gap and trial-state assumptions. Neutral large-N shell structure and correlated shell selection are not justified.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied complete definition, catalogue wording, scope qualifications and source list for this canonical ID. The original-source citations are inherited as supplied references; no fresh primary-source or novelty search was performed for this attempt. No current open-status claim is inferred from this calculation.

\paragraph{Atomic scope and a fixed-particle large-charge test.}The supplied repair identifies an atomic electron-shell programme despite the inherited nuclear title. In the stated kinetic normalization, dilate coordinates by \(y_i=Zx_i\). The unitary dilation gives
\[Z^{-2}H(N,Z)=H_0+Z^{-1}W,\quad H_0=\sum_{i=1}^N(-\Delta_{y_i}-|y_i|^{-1}),\quad W=\sum_{i<j}|y_i-y_j|^{-1}\ge0.\]
Keep \(N\) fixed. Let \(E_0\) be the infimum of \(H_0\) on the fermionic space. If \(\Phi\) is a normalized fermionic ground state of \(H_0\) with finite \(C=\langle\Phi,W\Phi\rangle\), the variational principle yields
\[E_0\le Z^{-2}\inf\sigma(H(N,Z))\le E_0+C/Z.\]
The assumptions on the trial state are explicit: one can use a Slater determinant of occupied bound one-electron orbitals, provided their Coulomb integrals are finite. No claim about a particular shell occupancy is needed for this conditional inequality.
\paragraph{A quantitative shell-subspace consequence.}Suppose additionally that \(H_0\) has a gap \(\Delta>0\) above its ground eigenspace, and denote its projection by \(P\). For a normalized ground state \(\Psi_Z\) of the interacting scaled Hamiltonian, positivity of \(W\) and the gap give
\[\Delta\|(I-P)\Psi_Z\|^2\le\langle\Psi_Z,(H_0-E_0)\Psi_Z\rangle\le C/Z.
\]
This proves concentration in the unperturbed ground subspace under the explicitly stated spectral assumptions. Degeneracy inside that subspace is allowed; the estimate does not pick an individual determinant.
\paragraph{First obstruction.}For neutral atoms \(N\) grows with \(Z\), so \(C\), shell gaps and degeneracies depend on \(N\); the fixed-\(N\) estimate supplies no uniform control. Within a degenerate shell, the interaction may select correlated states, which the projection bound cannot distinguish. A justified shell model for the exact atomic theory therefore needs uniform many-electron estimates and an effective dynamics or energy selection inside shell spaces. The elementary scaling test is not a nuclear model or a complete atomic-shell justification.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked the source correction to atomic physics, Coulomb scaling, positivity, gap projection inequality and failure of fixed-N constants to address neutral atoms. This was an LLM adversarial audit, not independent human verification; no theorem prover was used. Prompts A then B applied to this ID: Checked the source correction to atomic physics, Coulomb scaling, positivity, gap projection inequality and failure of fixed-N constants to address neutral atoms. Scope remains limited: Obtain estimates uniform as electron number grows and resolve interaction-induced dynamics inside degenerate shell subspaces.

Coordinator checked the fixed-particle dilation, positive interaction energy bounds and conditional spectral-gap projection estimate. No uniform neutral-atom conclusion is asserted. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

